feat(hook): keep a killed run's claim after it is retired (#291)

#290 made a killed run leave the active list at the moment of the kill, which
is right and was asked for. The destructive-git guard decides whose work to
protect by asking the ledger who is LIVE, so the work lost its protection at
the same instant. Measured on one tree, one piece of work, one run, against
the guard as it stood:

  before the retirement:  git clean -fd  ->  exit 2, refused
  after  the retirement:  git clean -fd  ->  exit 0, allowed, the file gone

Before #290 that work was protected for twelve hours, but only as a side
effect of the run being wrongly reported active. The side effect went and the
protection went with it -- and the work most at risk is exactly this work. A
live run's uncommitted work has somebody coming back for it. A killed run's
does not.

WHAT CARRIES THE CLAIM NOW is the record the kill already writes: reason
run_killed, naming the run and its tree. Two fields are added to it, because
what it named could neither protect nor release:

  claim      the paths the run left UNCOMMITTED, not every path it ever wrote
             there, with a digest of the bytes the kill left in each
  claim_top  the repository those paths are relative to

WHY THE CONTENT AND NOT THE NAME, which is the whole design. Liveness was
chosen in #278 because a killed subagent's marker is never removed (#260) and
a claim keyed on one would refuse in that tree forever. A claim keyed on a
FILENAME has the same defect one step further on: this record is append-only
and never rotated, so a claim that came back every time somebody re-edited a
path some long-dead run once wrote would end up claiming every file in the
repository. The claim is on the work.

SO IT RELEASES ITSELF, by three facts about the tree and no clock at all: the
path is committed, the path is gone, or the path has been written over. The
operator's existing override discards it once, deliberately, after which the
second rule applies. No threshold is added and the reaper's silence path and
grace are untouched.

AND IT IS NEVER A BLANKET CLAIM, which is the one place a killed run differs
from a live one. A live run whose body store cannot be read is refused over
every dirty path in the tree; a retired run cannot be given that, because the
record outlives every one of its paths and a blanket claim that outlives its
run is a tree nobody can use. Each destructive form is still weighed only
against what it can destroy: a tracked claim does not refuse clean -f and an
untracked one does not refuse reset --hard.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER is in the file: a record written before this existed,
which names no content and so claims nothing; any other reason in that log,
which records a run that did stop; a run whose writes went through Bash
redirections, which recorded no path to claim; and more than fifty paths from
one kill, which is the bound the record is written under.

Nothing reaches a tree the record does not name. One log serves every session
on the machine, and a record is weighed only where claim_top resolves to this
repository.

